      "requestAttempts": 0,
      "pronunciationText": "Here's a surprise. You might expect cooling to be what makes the crystals form. For many solids, it is. Potassium nitrate's solubility drops from about one hundred and sixty nine grams per hundred grams of water at eighty degrees, to about thirty one at twenty degrees. Cool a saturated solution over that range, and about a hundred and thirty eight grams crystallise. But salt barely changes. It's about thirty eight grams at eighty degrees, and about thirty six at twenty. So for salt, removing water is what forms most of the crystals, not cooling.",
